From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 20:20:45 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: cbm: follow the buffer manager's own device tree node

The node describing the packet buffer manager was reworked: its windows
are looked up by name, the window nothing ever mapped is gone, one name
lost an underscore, the segment pools were renamed, and the node now
carries two clocks of which the second is the gate.

Follow all of it. The window loop takes each window by name instead of
by position, which also removes the standing hazard that adding or
removing a window silently routes every later write elsewhere. The gate
clock is taken by name; without it the common clock framework switches
the datapath off again during late boot as an unused clock.

A processor's end of a switch macro's datapath owns no window of its own
and is described as a child of this node, so the children are given
their devices once the manager is up. The driver keeps a name of its
own rather than the device's: two platform drivers cannot share a name,
the conduit driver is called after the device it binds, and a second
registration under the same string aborts and leaves the conduit
unbound, with the switch then deferring on a conduit that never appears.

The list of datapath ports the manager preconfigures at probe - writing
each dequeue port's egress state and opening its transmit channel before
the controllers are enabled - now comes from the port children of the
switch macro nodes. A macro's port number is its datapath port number,
so the board already states which ports it wires; the ethernet node the
list used to come from is gone, and falling through to the built-in list
of the first macro's four ports would leave the second macro's
front-panel port without a dequeue port, without an egress path and with
its transmit channel closed, so a conduit serving that macro would have
nowhere to send a frame. The CPU port owns no dequeue port and is
skipped, as is any port number the tables cannot hold. A die that wires
no second-macro socket contributes nothing, so a board with one macro
yields the same four ports as before.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

